00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:11.190
And if you're all interested,
we can also talk at the end

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:15.120
and answer any questions that
people had around, you know,

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:17.730
AI performance at scale.

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:23.730
So since DeepSeek R1 gave teams
the recipe for RL post-training,

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:26.550
frontier quality reasoning
capability has been arriving

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:29.340
in open models every month.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:33.300
The gap between what you can
build and close API access

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.060
and what you can deploy
yourself has collapsed faster

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:38.370
than almost anyone predicted.

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:41.130
But a recipe is not the
same as infrastructure.

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:44.730
What R1 proved is that
RL post-training works.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:49.470
What it didn't hand teams
is what it takes to run it

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:52.920
and to run the
models it produces.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.410
That gap is where the
three of us are working.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:58.770
These techniques are elegant
in a paper and extensive

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:01.080
and expensive in practice.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.360
They land at a specific
layer of the stack

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.170
that most practitioners
never touch,

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:09.180
where the math meets
the memory hierarchy,

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:13.590
where abstraction choices show
up as GPU bills in production.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:17.520
The move that connects all
of it, trading generality

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:20.850
for performance through
specialization.

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:24.990
A general PyTorch optimization
works on any input,

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:28.620
any hardware, but that
generality has a cost.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:31.230
The moment you know exactly
what you are computing

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:35.220
and on exactly what hardware,
you can keep it in SRAM,

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:38.880
fuse the operations,
recompute instead of store.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.790
Same math, fewer round trips.

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:44.640
This is how a memory-bound
workload becomes a compute-bound

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:48.560
one, which is what GPUs
are actually good at.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:52.580
Daniel built Unsloth to make
the RL training loop runnable

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:54.350
on accessible hardware.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:56.480
Mark has spent
years at Kernel Air

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:59.190
and is now building
tools to automate it.

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:02.040
At Fireworks, we run a fully
managed inference service

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:03.840
on shared GPU clusters,

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.180
straddling these
trade-offs directly.

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:08.070
Diverse workloads,
different users,

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:09.810
multi-cloud configurations,

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:13.920
where every optimization
decision has a real cost.

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:16.860
So when it comes to
fine-tuning and post-training,

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.890
R1 proved that the
recipe actually worked.

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:22.080
What it didn't hand teams was
what it would actually take

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:23.370
to run it.

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:26.130
The frameworks most of us
started with were built

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:28.830
for supervised fine-tuning.

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:32.400
One pass through a fixed
dataset, stable memory access.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.220
When you move to RL, you're
generating sequences,

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:37.440
scoring them, updating
weights in a loop,

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:41.520
and those assumptions
stop holding.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:43.950
Daniel built Unsloth
specifically to go

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:46.770
after those bottlenecks,
getting RL training running

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:50.160
on hardware most teams
actually have access to.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:52.980
So Daniel, starting
from the ground up,

00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:55.170
what are gradient checkpointing

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.690
and memory layout
actually doing,

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:59.460
and where do those
assumptions fall apart

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:01.230
when you push into RL?

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:02.590
Daniel Han: Yeah.

00:06:03.806 --> 00:06:07.556
So I guess everyone knows
DeepSeek-R1 when it was --

00:06:07.590 --> 00:06:11.310
I think it was -- last January?
I think it was last January.

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:14.370
Rob Ferguson: Right.
Yeah, January 2025.

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:15.782
Because o1 came out in

00:06:15.815 --> 00:06:18.505
September 2024, and
we had what I would call

00:06:18.539 --> 00:06:20.295
"open model winter"
for four months.

00:06:20.329 --> 00:06:21.450
Daniel Han: -- open
source drought,

00:06:21.450 --> 00:06:23.220
that's what I called it. Yes,
"Open-source drought".

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:24.875
So like, after o1 got released,

00:06:24.908 --> 00:06:26.910
everyone was trying to clamor,

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:28.830
how does o1 do reasoning?

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:30.360
No one knew how to do it,

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:32.700
and so, from
September to January,

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:33.720
open-source kind of died.

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:35.520
Rob Ferguson: Everybody
went into a cave,

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:40.350
studied o1 all night and day,
until finally, I would say R1,

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:41.700
but really the paper, right?

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:42.510
Daniel Han: Yes, the paper.

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:43.470
Rob Ferguson: Yeah.

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:45.720
Daniel Han: I think it was
DeepSeek Math was the one

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:47.190
who introduced GRPO,

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:50.220
but essentially DeepSeek R1
was a large-scale training run

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:52.770
showing that GRPO
actually works very well.

00:06:52.770 --> 00:06:55.800
And I feel like so when the
algorithm got released,

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:58.320
it was still very hard to
run on, like, you know,

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:01.860
local computers, you know,
computers of less memory.

00:07:01.860 --> 00:07:04.290
And the reason why is because
reinforcement learning has two

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:05.370
different components now.

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:06.870
There is an inference component,

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:08.280
and there is a
training component.

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:10.560
And every single step,
you have to do inference,

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:12.810
you call the model to
generate your examples,

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:14.100
and then you do training.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:16.590
And then you do inference, and
then you do training, and then,

00:07:16.590 --> 00:07:18.300
you know, this loop
continuously goes on.

00:07:18.300 --> 00:07:19.680
And the problem
with that is, like,

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:22.890
you have two different engines
running at the same time;

00:07:22.890 --> 00:07:24.030
you have your inference engine,

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:25.590
and then you have
your training engine.

00:07:25.590 --> 00:07:27.930
And if you don't
do this correctly,

00:07:27.930 --> 00:07:29.670
you can balloon your
memory usage to be,

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:31.350
like, double, for example.

00:07:31.350 --> 00:07:33.930
So like, obviously, one very
easy trick is to, like,

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:36.870
you know, do the inference
run, offload the weights

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:39.810
of the inference weights, you
know, somewhere to, like, RAM,

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:41.400
and then do training next.

00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:43.740
And then you keep
doing this, you know,

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:45.450
some sort of
offloading approach.

00:07:45.450 --> 00:07:47.400
And this can, like, reduce
your memory usage by, like,

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:48.990
half, something like that.

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:50.070
Another way is,
like, for example,

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:52.320
with Unsloth what we do is we
do something called memory

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:53.940
weight sharing.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:57.270
So we use VLLM and, you know,
SGLang and other, like,

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:59.910
large inference,
high-throughput frameworks.

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:01.560
You can also use
Llama CPP and others.

00:08:02.107 --> 00:08:03.801
And the trick is,

00:08:03.834 --> 00:08:06.240
you load the inference engine,

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:07.860
and then during the
training stage,

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:10.650
we share the weights directly
with the inference engine.

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:12.990
You can only do this,
though, for LoRA,

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:14.460
so you can't do this for,
like, full fine-tuning.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:15.840
It's a bit more complicated.

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:18.420
But for LoRA, this
works very, very well.

00:08:18.420 --> 00:08:21.990
And you can reduce memory
usage dramatically, like,

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:24.690
50% or more by just
doing this approach.

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:26.340
And there's many
other tricks, like,

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:28.530
there's something called
standby in sleep.

00:08:28.530 --> 00:08:30.000
So essentially that's the
thing that I described,

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:34.770
where you offload the inference
engine to, like, RAM,

00:08:34.770 --> 00:08:36.150
and then you bring it back up.

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:39.810
So you, you know, let it
come back from standby.

00:08:39.810 --> 00:08:41.820
And that's another
thing that you can do.

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:43.650
And your gradient
checkpointing is a very --

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:46.800
so gradient checkpointing, I
think it was invented by OpenAI,

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:48.120
or maybe I'm getting confused.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:49.500
There was a paper by OpenAI.

00:08:49.500 --> 00:08:49.830
Rob Ferguson: It was.

00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:52.350
I think it was, like, Yaroslav
Bulatov at like, OpenAI.

00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:53.190
Yes, that's correct.

00:08:53.190 --> 00:08:53.700
Daniel Han: Okay.

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:54.990
A guy from OpenAI.

00:08:54.990 --> 00:08:55.380
Rob Ferguson: Yaroslav.

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:56.010
Daniel Han: Very famous.

00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:56.940
Yes, yes, yes.

00:08:56.940 --> 00:08:59.880
A guy from OpenAI invented
gradient checkpointing.

00:08:59.880 --> 00:09:01.290
But, yeah, gradient
checkpointing is extremely

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:03.630
important for, you know,
for reinforcement learning,

00:09:03.630 --> 00:09:05.910
for fine-tuning, for
every single algorithm.

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:08.190
And the trick is, you know,
instead of trying to compute,

00:09:08.190 --> 00:09:10.320
instead of trying to
save all the activations

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:13.650
for every single, you know,
tensor, every single layer,

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:14.910
we just recompute them

00:09:14.910 --> 00:09:17.130
on the fly during the
forward pass and the back.

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:19.200
Okay, that's a bit more slower.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:22.800
But, you know, the main goal is,
like, every single optimization

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:26.670
and algorithm that we, like,
use, it can compound, like,

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:29.730
it can compound and multiply
and make the process, like,

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:31.350
you know, much more efficient.

00:09:31.350 --> 00:09:33.210
So yeah, everything that you
described is very important

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:35.220
for reinforcement learning,
fine-tuning, and everything.

00:09:35.220 --> 00:09:35.940
Yeah.

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:36.210
Rob Ferguson: Yeah,

00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:38.970
and there's so many details
to this, of course.

00:09:38.970 --> 00:09:39.150
Daniel Han: Yes.

00:09:39.150 --> 00:09:41.400
Rob Ferguson: Because bugs
can be introduced everywhere

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:42.960
when you're actually doing it.

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:45.000
When you have a mixture
of experts, of course,

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:48.690
each expert could be going from
a different point in time.

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:50.940
With floating-point operations,

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:53.010
the same floating-point
operation might not recompute

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:54.570
the same way twice.

00:09:54.570 --> 00:10:00.150
So to actually think about doing
this in a way that is going

00:10:00.150 --> 00:10:01.470
to be resilient and something

00:10:01.470 --> 00:10:05.010
that you can have reproducible
results is a lot more difficult

00:10:05.010 --> 00:10:07.620
when starting to think
about this actually

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:10.920
for specific hardware
or specific scale.

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:11.780
Daniel Han: Yeah.

00:10:11.780 --> 00:10:14.000
No, like, even, like, you know,
even the GPU itself, like,

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:15.990
you know, when you do
matrix multiplication,

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:17.280
it's not the same, right?

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:20.370
So like, you can schedule
this kernel to run

00:10:20.370 --> 00:10:21.840
and another kernel differently.

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:23.520
And, like, you need
to be very careful.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:25.500
Like, you know, how do
we do deterministic

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:26.340
reinforcement learning?

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:28.680
That is a very, you know,
important topic as well.

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:30.960
Sometimes your reinforcement
learning runs are not the same.

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:33.000
You know, it looks the
same, but then you run it

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:35.820
for a little bit more, and
then it's, like, different.

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:36.990
And it's hard to be
doing, you know,

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:40.170
reproducible reinforcement
learning and stuff like that.

00:10:40.170 --> 00:10:43.020
Yeah. I guess these are
very problematic problem

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:44.310
that needs to be solved.

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:45.210
Rob Ferguson: Yeah.

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:47.460
Well, and these are
non-deterministic systems.

00:10:47.460 --> 00:10:49.920
So even being able to
verify your outputs,

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:52.080
although for some things
like math and coding,

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:56.700
it's a little easier to have
very strong evaluation cases,

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:58.770
be able to have things
that you can work against.

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:00.150
Maybe it's worth
talking a little bit

00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:02.460
about what GRPO is
for the audience.

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:02.700
Daniel Han: Yeah.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:09.090
So when OpenAI first
released ChatGPT,

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:10.800
and then they introduced PPO.

00:11:10.800 --> 00:11:12.180
Is that correct?

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:13.380
I'm pretty sure that's correct.

00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.270
So they introduced PPO
as part of a paper?

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:16.380
Mark Saroufim: I
think GRPO is part

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:19.950
of the DeepSeek paper, I think.

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:21.720
Rob Ferguson: I think
PPO was John Shulman.

00:11:21.720 --> 00:11:23.310
I think he did this
during his PhD.

00:11:23.310 --> 00:11:23.790
Daniel Han: Oh, okay, fine.

00:11:23.790 --> 00:11:24.360
Rob Ferguson: This
was before that.

00:11:24.360 --> 00:11:25.470
Daniel Han: John
Shulman did PPO.

00:11:25.470 --> 00:11:29.160
Fine. So correction, yes.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:32.280
And so PPO was used for training
of large models like ChatGPT.

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:33.750
Rob Ferguson: Yeah,
very, very simple.

00:11:33.750 --> 00:11:34.200
Daniel Han: Yeah, yeah.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:36.630
Just positive reward, negative
reward, just like, you know,

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:38.100
asking humans, is
this a good example,

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:39.420
is this a negative example?

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:40.860
And it was very,
very, very useful.

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:43.500
But the problem is there's
too many components of PPO.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:43.740
Rob Ferguson: Yeah.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:46.260
Daniel Han: There's a value
model, there's a reward model.

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:49.380
And then GRPO came along and
just deleted the reward model

00:11:49.380 --> 00:11:51.930
and deleted the value
model and replaced it

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:55.260
by instead calling the
model to generate examples.

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:55.410
Rob Ferguson: Right.

00:11:55.410 --> 00:11:57.180
Daniel Han: And then when you
generate examples, you can,

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:59.100
like, have a
distribution, right?

00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:01.050
You take, you minus the
mean, you divide it

00:12:01.050 --> 00:12:04.500
by the standard deviation,
and then you use this instead

00:12:04.500 --> 00:12:06.840
as the value model
and the reward model.

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:09.540
So for the reward model, you
will create reward functions.

00:12:09.540 --> 00:12:11.790
So you will say, okay,
is this question good?

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:14.760
Is this, you know, is the answer
to this question good or bad?

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:16.620
Like, you know,
what is 2 plus 2?

00:12:16.620 --> 00:12:17.610
The answer is 4.

00:12:17.610 --> 00:12:18.990
So 4 is the correct answer.

00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:21.090
But, you know, like,
"cat" is a bad answer.

00:12:21.090 --> 00:12:23.520
Right. And so, you will reward,
you know, "cat" as, like,

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:26.970
minus 100 and 4 as,
like, plus 100.

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:27.720
Rob Ferguson: Yeah.

00:12:27.720 --> 00:12:29.340
Daniel Han: And so, by
doing this, you know,

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:31.890
that's where GRPO
and RLVR comes in.

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:34.020
So RLVR stands for
Reinforcement Learning

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:35.310
with Verifiable Rewards.

00:12:35.310 --> 00:12:35.550
Rob Ferguson: Yes.

00:12:35.550 --> 00:12:38.220
Daniel Han: And once you
have this verifiable rewards

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:41.550
and an optimized
approach, you know,

00:12:41.550 --> 00:12:42.750
that's where GRPO comes in.

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:46.740
It's just, so, GRPO is an
optimized version of PPO

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:49.200
and the other reinforcement
learning algorithms.

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:50.430
Rob Ferguson: For those
who aren't as familiar

00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:53.430
with reinforcement learning,
I think just this idea

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:54.570
that there are some things

00:12:54.570 --> 00:12:58.530
that intrinsically can have
a score is really useful.

00:12:58.530 --> 00:13:02.850
You know, before reinforcement
learning came through, you know,

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:06.360
you had spaces like video
compression where, like,

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:10.380
maybe you'd have 100 PhDs work
to get a very small percentage

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:12.570
of video compression to improve.

00:13:12.570 --> 00:13:14.790
But the second that you could
have a score, you could go

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:18.180
and say, okay, I'm going to
keep rewarding the system

00:13:18.180 --> 00:13:20.790
if it's able to go and
to increase the score.

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:23.250
And then, immediately,
video compression,

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:26.790
4% better immediately
after that same run.

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:28.320
And so, this idea of being able

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:31.110
to score your outputs is really
powerful, and we find all

00:13:31.110 --> 00:13:33.870
over different parts of machine
learning much more useful

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:36.600
than just general preferences,
where this is kind of good,

00:13:36.600 --> 00:13:38.940
this is kind of bad, because you
can start hill climbing again,

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:42.540
so you can actually start
having more intelligence

00:13:42.540 --> 00:13:47.280
with actual things that
are easier to program for.

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:50.520
So I guess my question for
you is, what is the mismatch

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:52.470
between current
frameworks assume

00:13:52.470 --> 00:13:56.700
and what RL actually needs,
still causing the most pain

00:13:56.700 --> 00:14:01.060
for teams who are trying to ship
and use RL for the first time?

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:07.740
Daniel Han: So the biggest
problem of RL is, in my view,

00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:10.110
it's the reward functions
that is the problem.

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:12.720
How do we define the
correct reward functions?

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:14.250
How do we actually
understand the problem

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:15.330
that you're trying to solve?

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:17.460
How do we, like,
hill-climb the reward?

00:14:17.460 --> 00:14:20.070
You know, like, your goal
is to maximize reward,

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:22.920
but how do you define the
reward is the problem.

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:26.520
So most labs, for example,
you want to, like, play,

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:30.270
you want to do a general system,
you know, one that can solve Go,

00:14:30.270 --> 00:14:34.500
play League of Legends, do
weather prediction, solve maths,

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:36.270
everything in this one model.

00:14:36.270 --> 00:14:38.970
And so, like, the question is,
for each of these systems,

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:41.820
you know, like, Go, what
is your reward function?

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:43.770
For your maths one, you know,
what is the reward function?

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:48.360
And so, you need to define a
combined reward function system

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:51.210
that can, like -- it shouldn't
compete with each other.

00:14:51.210 --> 00:14:52.710
Like, you also need
to, like, weight them.

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:55.440
You know, is the reward
function for Go more important

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:57.720
than the reward function for
maths or something like that?

00:14:57.720 --> 00:15:01.320
And so, like, there's many
problems with the orchestration

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:03.570
of how you actually do
reinforcement learning.

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:05.940
So like, you know, defining
the reward function, you know,

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:08.010
like, do you do it binary?

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:09.870
Like, you know, is
it zero and one?

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:11.400
Zero is good reward.

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:12.870
One is positive reward.

00:15:12.870 --> 00:15:14.160
Or do you do a linear scale?

00:15:14.160 --> 00:15:16.410
You know, minus 100 to 100.

00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:20.490
And so, like, it's
more like an art.

00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:23.310
And I think that's where they,
you know, people in the audience

00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:24.840
and us, you know, we
all have to, like,

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:27.510
define these reward functions.

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:29.040
Obviously, the large
labs, my guess,

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:30.600
is they will automate
this as well.

00:15:30.600 --> 00:15:33.330
You know, their goal is to make
automatic reward functions,

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:37.290
make automatic environments,
make everything automatic.

00:15:37.290 --> 00:15:38.940
And then they could
do some, you know,

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:41.340
their goal is to
scale that to AGI.

00:15:41.340 --> 00:15:43.140
That's another way you
can think about it.

00:15:43.140 --> 00:15:45.840
But my take is, like, you
know, we need to, like,

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:48.030
assign rewards correctly.

00:15:48.030 --> 00:15:50.160
And that is a very --
like, a good example.

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:51.900
You do a self-driving car.

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:54.030
You do not want to
kill any humans.

00:15:54.030 --> 00:15:58.020
And so, you should assign
negative infinity to, you know,

00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:00.480
if it, yeah, kills a
human, for example, right?

00:16:00.480 --> 00:16:01.860
You assign negative infinity.

00:16:01.860 --> 00:16:02.460
Rob Ferguson: Right.

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:04.560
Daniel Han: But that's
a very easy example.

00:16:04.560 --> 00:16:06.540
But, like, for other examples,
it's more harder to, like,

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:09.300
define reward, like, what
is good, what is bad.

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:11.580
And it's more like human
intuition, you know,

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:13.980
this is good, this is
bad, and stuff like that.

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:14.580
Yeah.

00:16:14.580 --> 00:16:17.910
Rob Ferguson: Although I've
definitely seen people, like,

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:19.410
use Claude to build the reward.

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:20.160
Daniel Han: Oh, yes, yes, yes.

00:16:20.160 --> 00:16:21.660
No, we do that as well.

00:16:21.660 --> 00:16:23.730
Like, you know, you can
ask Claude, you know,

00:16:23.730 --> 00:16:26.280
you can ask open-source models,
build a reward function,

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:30.630
build the environment, but
probably verify, you know.

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:33.030
And so, like, best to verify
before you, like, input this

00:16:33.030 --> 00:16:33.960
and start, like, a training run.

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:34.320
Yeah.

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:36.870
Rob Ferguson: And so, Mark,
as our automation expert,

00:16:36.870 --> 00:16:39.120
are these going to
be fully automated?

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:46.560
Are we going to automate
all RL rewards?

00:16:46.560 --> 00:16:49.380
Mark Saroufim: Yeah, like,
will we automate all RL?

00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:53.520
I think yes, but not,
like, naively, basically.

00:16:53.520 --> 00:16:56.310
So I've been becoming, like,
increasingly convinced that,

00:16:56.310 --> 00:17:00.060
like, I view it almost like as
this adversarial self-play game

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:02.700
between an AI model
that's trying

00:17:02.700 --> 00:17:03.990
to build the best
thing possible.

00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:04.890
Like, for instance,
let's say it's trying

00:17:04.890 --> 00:17:07.080
to build the fastest
GPU code possible.

00:17:07.080 --> 00:17:09.600
And then another model that's
trying to figure out if

00:17:09.600 --> 00:17:11.670
that other AI model is cheating.

00:17:11.670 --> 00:17:13.440
And ideally, you sort
of view this as a game

00:17:13.440 --> 00:17:16.710
because as the base
model gets smarter,

00:17:16.710 --> 00:17:18.630
it also gets smarter
at cheating.

00:17:18.630 --> 00:17:22.230
And now your evaluation code is
more prone to reward hacking.

00:17:22.230 --> 00:17:24.180
But then the auditor
is not going

00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:26.940
to get smarter unless
it's also collaborating

00:17:26.940 --> 00:17:27.990
with a smart cheater.

00:17:27.990 --> 00:17:28.260
Rob Ferguson: Right.

00:17:28.260 --> 00:17:31.470
Mark Saroufim: So I view this
like this, but this is, like,

00:17:31.470 --> 00:17:34.110
this is not great news
in the sense that, like,

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:36.600
even when people had these sort
of observations in the past

00:17:36.600 --> 00:17:39.000
when we're doing
self-play on video games,

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:40.860
the models would
conspire, basically.

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:42.750
They would sort of say, well,
I don't think they're cheating

00:17:42.750 --> 00:17:44.850
because they want
the aggregate reward

00:17:44.850 --> 00:17:46.590
of both systems to be good.

00:17:46.590 --> 00:17:49.710
And so, like, you still need to
often define a single reward.

00:17:49.710 --> 00:17:51.960
But, like, one of my guesses,
like, at least, like,

00:17:51.960 --> 00:17:54.300
decoupling those two
things would help

00:17:54.300 --> 00:17:55.980
automation significantly.

00:17:55.980 --> 00:17:57.840
I will also say,
like, a lot of things

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:00.540
that people think are
verifiable are not quite

00:18:00.540 --> 00:18:02.610
as verifiable as they seem.

00:18:02.610 --> 00:18:04.140
For example, coding.

00:18:04.140 --> 00:18:06.300
You're like, oh, there's
a right answer or not.

00:18:06.300 --> 00:18:09.450
Well, one way of having a right
answer is it produces the right

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:11.790
output, and it doesn't crash.

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:12.930
Seems reasonable.

00:18:12.930 --> 00:18:16.530
But, like, let's say, like, you
ask the AI model to do this,

00:18:16.530 --> 00:18:18.270
but then it just generates,
like, armies of,

00:18:18.270 --> 00:18:19.320
like, try-accept blocks.

00:18:19.320 --> 00:18:20.130
You know, we've all seen them.

00:18:20.130 --> 00:18:21.240
We don't like them.

00:18:21.240 --> 00:18:22.410
So this is not verifiable.

00:18:22.410 --> 00:18:25.700
Now we're saying, like, I want
you to give the correct code,

00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:29.780
but don't be overly defensive
or don't be overly verbose

00:18:29.780 --> 00:18:31.730
or don't, like,
reinvent the function

00:18:31.730 --> 00:18:33.950
that already exists
in the code base.

00:18:33.950 --> 00:18:35.390
Like, all of a sudden,
it becomes, like,

00:18:35.390 --> 00:18:36.410
a lot more nuanced, like,

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:38.700
what we actually want
from these AI models.

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:40.470
So I suspect that if we, like,

00:18:40.470 --> 00:18:42.750
at least acknowledge
these problems,

00:18:42.750 --> 00:18:45.600
and then we build AI
systems such that, like,

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:46.770
they learn from experience.

00:18:46.770 --> 00:18:51.600
They learn that, okay, I build
a prototype, I de-slopify it.

00:18:51.600 --> 00:18:54.664
I show this prototype to
users, users tell me what

00:18:54.697 --> 00:18:56.915
forward-facing --
what API they like,

00:18:56.949 --> 00:19:01.020
I reduce the complexity
of the code, then yes.

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:03.510
But I think continual
learning, in some sense, like,

00:19:03.510 --> 00:19:05.820
will happen, but
it's also, I think,

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:08.220
harder than people
think they are.

00:19:08.220 --> 00:19:09.150
I would say, like,

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:11.130
the philosophy Daniel
described is sort

00:19:11.130 --> 00:19:13.920
of the dominant philosophy
of just have more

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:17.640
and more RL environments
and we'll get to AGI.

00:19:17.640 --> 00:19:21.600
I'm suspicious that this
will work, but it does seem

00:19:21.600 --> 00:19:23.430
to work better than
you might expect,

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:28.350
but I just don't think this is
the recipe for AGI quite yet.

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:28.800
Daniel Han: Agreed.

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:31.140
I was going to say I agree.

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:32.730
I think, like, you
can get somewhere.

00:19:32.730 --> 00:19:35.610
So like, I think, like, the
trick of, like, you know,

00:19:35.610 --> 00:19:39.030
large model training and, like,
pre-training was you have so,

00:19:39.030 --> 00:19:42.630
so, so, so, so many examples
and pretend you, like,

00:19:42.630 --> 00:19:44.160
remove maths from the equation.

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:45.660
Pretend you, like, get
that pre-training data set

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:47.870
and you just remove
the maths from it.

00:19:47.870 --> 00:19:50.090
Most likely, it will
fill that hole.

00:19:50.090 --> 00:19:50.780
Most likely.

00:19:50.780 --> 00:19:51.830
Like, I'm not sure.

00:19:51.830 --> 00:19:52.970
Has anyone actually
done the experiment?

00:19:52.970 --> 00:19:53.690
I'm actually not sure.

00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:55.190
But, like, most
likely for the hole.

00:19:55.190 --> 00:19:57.410
So I think, like, with these
large runs and many, many,

00:19:57.410 --> 00:19:59.580
many environments, you
can get somewhere,

00:19:59.580 --> 00:20:00.720
but I actually agree with you.

00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:03.890
You can only get somewhere, but
then if you want to reach more,

00:20:03.890 --> 00:20:06.350
then, okay, you need
to do something more.

00:20:06.350 --> 00:20:06.950
And you never know.

00:20:06.950 --> 00:20:09.050
Maybe the reward actually,
as you said, reward hacking.

00:20:09.050 --> 00:20:10.844
The reward might be
going up, but actually,

00:20:10.880 --> 00:20:11.930
it's actually going down.

00:20:11.930 --> 00:20:15.000
Like, it's actually, like,
hacking, doing lots of, like,

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:17.670
shifty behavior, you
know, editing the timer.

00:20:17.670 --> 00:20:19.470
You know, if you want to make
faster matrix multiplication

00:20:19.470 --> 00:20:21.720
kernels, just edit
the timer to be zero.

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:25.050
Or, you know, making the
matrix just all zeros.

00:20:25.050 --> 00:20:27.300
And you never actually
know, like, you know,

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:28.290
for every single example.

00:20:28.290 --> 00:20:29.910
You can't check
every single one.

00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:30.720
So I agree with you.

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:31.530
Mark Saroufim: Yeah.

00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:33.690
So I think part of the challenge
here is that, like, indeed,

00:20:33.690 --> 00:20:35.520
people are sort of very
bullish on this vision

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:37.830
of just automate
the environments.

00:20:37.830 --> 00:20:40.380
But then, like, what I'm also
sort of observing is that, like,

00:20:40.380 --> 00:20:42.300
you know, when I joined
Core Auto, I'm, like,

00:20:42.300 --> 00:20:43.680
I'm going to be
doing, like, math.

00:20:43.680 --> 00:20:45.540
I'm going to be writing
state-of-the-art systems.

00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:48.180
I find myself, like, labeling
data quite a bit today.

00:20:48.180 --> 00:20:48.750
Daniel Han: Oh, yes.

00:20:48.750 --> 00:20:50.220
Mark Saroufim: Which
is, like, just to see.

00:20:50.220 --> 00:20:51.000
Daniel Han: Fun, fun, fun.

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:51.870
Mark Saroufim: Yeah, it's fun.

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:54.300
But at least, like, I
think once you have, like,

00:20:54.300 --> 00:20:58.080
a human core label
egregious reward hacks,

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:01.380
you can still have the AI models
use that as, like, a label sort

00:21:01.380 --> 00:21:04.770
of golden data set of, like,
what reward hacks might be.

00:21:04.770 --> 00:21:07.320
And you can make your
infrastructure more robust.

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:10.110
And so, it is, like, data
labeling, but it's a form of,

00:21:10.110 --> 00:21:13.200
like, scalable data labeling
because the AI gets it.

00:21:13.200 --> 00:21:16.050
They're, like, oh, I see what
you mean by reward hacking.

00:21:16.050 --> 00:21:19.290
Let me just, like, eliminate
this entire class of bugs.

00:21:19.290 --> 00:21:21.810
Python is a very interesting
language, basically.

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:23.370
It's interesting
because it's, like,

00:21:23.370 --> 00:21:27.180
it's actually the worst
choice for AGI because, like,

00:21:27.180 --> 00:21:30.674
Python has, like,
it has "duct-taping",

00:21:30.707 --> 00:21:31.920
it has "monkey-patching".

00:21:31.920 --> 00:21:32.190
Daniel Han: Yeah.

00:21:32.190 --> 00:21:34.140
Mark Saroufim: So you can,
like, let's say I'm trying

00:21:34.140 --> 00:21:35.700
to benchmark a function,

00:21:35.700 --> 00:21:38.310
that function can change
the benchmark code.

00:21:38.310 --> 00:21:40.740
Like, it can use the
Python garbage collector,

00:21:40.740 --> 00:21:43.290
the Python inspect module.

00:21:43.290 --> 00:21:46.170
It can just, like, use, like,
dictionary direct assignments.

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:48.990
And there's, like, not much you
can do beyond telling it, like,

00:21:48.990 --> 00:21:51.750
don't do that, but
then it still does it.

00:21:51.750 --> 00:21:53.970
And unless you're looking
at it, you will, like,

00:21:53.970 --> 00:21:56.790
keep fooling yourself,
like, over and over again.

00:21:56.790 --> 00:21:59.430
So yeah, the models are
superhuman at cheating,

00:21:59.430 --> 00:22:01.200
but they're
superhuman at coding.

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:02.730
I think both are true.

00:22:02.730 --> 00:22:05.010
And I think, like, once you sort
of realize this, you, like,

00:22:05.010 --> 00:22:07.080
get enlightened, and you
can, like, figure out,

00:22:07.080 --> 00:22:08.730
like, the right recipe.

00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:09.270
Rob Ferguson: I
think that's part

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:12.420
of the reason why all AI
researchers start learning

00:22:12.420 --> 00:22:14.820
by watching models
self-play in video games.

00:22:14.820 --> 00:22:17.220
Because watching a model cheat
in a video game gives you, like,

00:22:17.220 --> 00:22:19.980
a really clear sense of
how they're cheating,

00:22:19.980 --> 00:22:22.620
where you see them do all sorts
of funny things where it's like,

00:22:22.620 --> 00:22:24.360
oh, there was an error
in the game engine

00:22:24.360 --> 00:22:26.520
where you could shift
a pixel or something.

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:28.830
Anything you can imagine,
they'll find it eventually.

00:22:28.830 --> 00:22:29.070
Mark Saroufim: Yes.

00:22:29.070 --> 00:22:30.420
Rob Ferguson: Right?

00:22:30.420 --> 00:22:34.470
And even things that
seem incredibly verifiable.

00:22:34.470 --> 00:22:36.810
So like, verifying
JSON output seems

00:22:36.810 --> 00:22:39.180
like something you could
give a very simple score to,

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:42.090
but in practice, much,
much more difficult.

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:43.800
I don't know of
anybody who has a

00:22:43.833 --> 00:22:47.850
perfectly verifiable
JSON as a --

00:22:47.850 --> 00:22:49.650
Mark Saroufim: So,
I have an example.

00:22:49.650 --> 00:22:51.274
I love coding so

00:22:51.307 --> 00:22:54.150
as part of GPU-mode stuff,
we run kernel competitions

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:56.910
where people compete on
writing fast GPU code.

00:22:56.910 --> 00:22:59.340
One of the AI models
discovered a reward hack

00:22:59.340 --> 00:23:02.850
that was previously done
by Volkswagen engineers.

00:23:02.850 --> 00:23:04.680
So some of you might have
heard of "Dieselgate",

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:07.560
which was basically this
conspiracy of, like,

00:23:07.560 --> 00:23:09.330
under emission testing,

00:23:09.330 --> 00:23:12.330
Volkswagen cars were
having 40x less emissions

00:23:12.330 --> 00:23:13.920
than on the actual road.

00:23:13.920 --> 00:23:16.170
So the AI models figured
out something very similar.

00:23:16.170 --> 00:23:18.330
When we were testing
them for correctness,

00:23:18.330 --> 00:23:20.190
they would give us
a correct kernel.

00:23:20.190 --> 00:23:22.200
But then they would figure
out when we're testing them

00:23:22.200 --> 00:23:24.990
for performance and then
give us an incorrect kernel

00:23:24.990 --> 00:23:26.280
that was, like, very fast.

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:27.930
So this is, like, what
we're up against.

00:23:27.930 --> 00:23:30.810
And we're going to rediscover
this rich literature

00:23:30.810 --> 00:23:32.640
of engineers cheating
throughout, like,

00:23:32.640 --> 00:23:34.830
our entire field's discipline.

00:23:34.830 --> 00:23:36.120
And so, this has been
really fun for me

00:23:36.120 --> 00:23:38.220
to rediscover these things.

00:23:38.220 --> 00:23:39.330
Rob Ferguson: Yeah, absolutely.

00:23:39.330 --> 00:23:42.870
You know, I used to make
a device that would plug

00:23:42.870 --> 00:23:45.060
into the OBD2 part of the car,

00:23:45.060 --> 00:23:48.000
and you would see that all of
the hacking that they would do

00:23:48.000 --> 00:23:51.780
on the inside bus
just to do that.

00:23:51.780 --> 00:23:54.780
Amazing that everything comes
back to the same sets of cheats.

00:23:54.780 --> 00:23:57.150
But you mentioned LoRA before.

00:23:57.150 --> 00:23:58.530
And I feel like a lot
of people aren't going

00:23:58.530 --> 00:24:01.320
to know very much about LoRA.

00:24:01.320 --> 00:24:04.590
And so, maybe we can start off
just by describing what that is.

00:24:04.590 --> 00:24:05.550
Daniel Han: Okay, yeah.

00:24:05.550 --> 00:24:09.120
So when you do training or
fine-tuning of a model,

00:24:09.120 --> 00:24:11.160
you can have, like,
many different options.

00:24:11.160 --> 00:24:13.560
The first option is you
take the entire model

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:15.540
and train every
single weight of it.

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:17.910
So like, you know, every single
weight becomes updatable.

00:24:17.910 --> 00:24:19.650
You will use, like,
backpropagation,

00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:23.070
differentiation calculus to,
like, update all these weights.

00:24:23.070 --> 00:24:25.440
The other approach is you
don't need to do that.

00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:27.570
Instead of training
the entire model,

00:24:27.570 --> 00:24:30.540
you can add very small
slither of weights.

00:24:30.540 --> 00:24:33.750
So it's like an "A" matrix
times a small "B" matrix

00:24:33.750 --> 00:24:35.310
and you multiply them together,

00:24:35.310 --> 00:24:37.890
and you add it to
the large model,

00:24:37.890 --> 00:24:38.550
and you do this, like,

00:24:38.550 --> 00:24:40.530
every single tensor,
every single layer.

00:24:40.530 --> 00:24:43.620
And essentially, you don't need
to update every single layer.

00:24:43.620 --> 00:24:45.390
You don't need to update
every single weight.

00:24:45.390 --> 00:24:47.280
You now only need
to update, like,

00:24:47.280 --> 00:24:49.710
1% of extra embedded weights.

00:24:49.710 --> 00:24:52.710
So if your model was, like,
1 trillion parameters,

00:24:52.710 --> 00:24:54.870
you don't need to do every
single 1 trillion parameter

00:24:54.870 --> 00:24:55.650
weight update.

00:24:55.650 --> 00:24:58.860
You only need to do a
very small amount of it.

00:24:58.860 --> 00:24:59.730
And the reason why we want

00:24:59.730 --> 00:25:02.490
to do this is training can
be very, very expensive.

00:25:02.490 --> 00:25:04.800
If you want to actually
do a large training run,

00:25:04.800 --> 00:25:07.590
you should still do it
but as an experiment.

00:25:07.623 --> 00:25:09.608
Or, like, you know, if
you want to deploy to, like,

00:25:09.642 --> 00:25:12.690
many, many users with each
different specific fine-tune,

00:25:12.690 --> 00:25:14.640
you should do LoRA.

00:25:14.640 --> 00:25:17.580
And this allows you to, like,
train many, many, many,

00:25:17.580 --> 00:25:21.210
larger models with
much less memory usage,

00:25:21.210 --> 00:25:23.370
and the accuracy
is also very good.

00:25:23.370 --> 00:25:25.200
So the Thinking Machines,
we did a collaboration

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:28.290
with Thinking Machines on a
blog post, "LoRA About Regret".

00:25:28.290 --> 00:25:30.180
And they showed in the blog post

00:25:30.180 --> 00:25:34.950
that if you do a rank of "1",
so you only need 1 column

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:38.075
of numbers times
a row of numbers

00:25:38.109 --> 00:25:39.750
for every single tensor,

00:25:39.750 --> 00:25:42.283
it still works well,

00:25:42.317 --> 00:25:43.380
but there is a caveat.

00:25:43.380 --> 00:25:45.390
You need to be careful of
the numbers that you select.

00:25:45.390 --> 00:25:47.610
For example, the learning
rates must be 10 times larger

00:25:47.610 --> 00:25:50.940
than normal, so "2E" minus "4".

00:25:50.940 --> 00:25:54.630
The LoRA rank, you also
need to be very careful.

00:25:54.630 --> 00:25:56.490
So when you
choose a rank of "1",

00:25:56.490 --> 00:25:58.470
the LoRA alpha needs
to be also selected.

00:25:58.470 --> 00:26:00.870
So, the scaling also
needs to be changed.

00:26:00.870 --> 00:26:04.890
And the blog post showed that
even a rank of "1", so very,

00:26:04.890 --> 00:26:08.610
very small, works very well
for specific use cases.

00:26:08.610 --> 00:26:11.100
Like, for example, for
maths, for coding,

00:26:11.100 --> 00:26:13.170
some examples of coding,

00:26:13.170 --> 00:26:14.550
not in general.

00:26:14.550 --> 00:26:17.040
But essentially, if you
want to do LoRA training,

00:26:17.040 --> 00:26:20.010
even a rank of
"1" works very well.

00:26:20.010 --> 00:26:21.240
Rob Ferguson: So
just to elaborate,

00:26:21.240 --> 00:26:24.420
rank "8 "would still be
a massive reduction.

00:26:24.420 --> 00:26:25.770
Daniel Han: Yes.

00:26:25.770 --> 00:26:27.900
Rob Ferguson: And
so, rank "1" is almost

00:26:27.900 --> 00:26:29.700
like just being
like, it's that way.

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:32.430
It's like only applying a
direction would be another way

00:26:32.430 --> 00:26:33.360
to describe it.

00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:34.260
And the fact that that works

00:26:34.260 --> 00:26:36.930
at all is absolutely
stunning to me. The --

00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:38.220
Daniel Han: Actually,
that's actually interesting

00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:40.710
because there's actually two
schools of thought of models.

00:26:40.710 --> 00:26:44.250
The first thought is like models
already have innate knowledge.

00:26:44.250 --> 00:26:48.780
So essentially, the reason
why these rank "1" works is,

00:26:48.780 --> 00:26:50.280
as what you said, it's
just a direction.

00:26:50.280 --> 00:26:54.750
Like, you know, you're forcing
the behavior out of the model.

00:26:54.750 --> 00:26:54.960
Rob Ferguson: Right.

00:26:54.960 --> 00:26:56.250
Daniel Han: And so, there's
actually a school of thought

00:26:56.250 --> 00:26:58.170
which thinks that
all these models

00:26:58.170 --> 00:27:01.050
and reinforcement learning
is doing is, you know,

00:27:01.050 --> 00:27:03.210
accentuating the
facts from the model.

00:27:03.210 --> 00:27:03.960
And then the other school

00:27:03.960 --> 00:27:05.730
of thought is reinforcement
learning is actually learning

00:27:05.730 --> 00:27:07.080
something new.

00:27:07.080 --> 00:27:09.300
And my take is
like it's halfway.

00:27:09.300 --> 00:27:12.090
You know, like, it is learning
definitely something new

00:27:12.090 --> 00:27:14.190
if you give them more
and more examples.

00:27:14.190 --> 00:27:16.440
But if you just want to, like,
accentuate something, like,

00:27:16.440 --> 00:27:20.010
you know, point it in a specific
direction, you can also do that.

00:27:20.010 --> 00:27:22.590
And so, like, we're,
like, halfway in between.

00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:24.060
Rob Ferguson: You know, what's
so interesting for me is,

00:27:24.060 --> 00:27:27.060
like, we're talking often
about, like, less than 2%

00:27:27.060 --> 00:27:28.950
of the weights of
the model changing.

00:27:28.950 --> 00:27:30.390
So like, it's something
incredibly small.

00:27:30.390 --> 00:27:31.410
Daniel Han: You're
going to be doing 0.5?

00:27:31.410 --> 00:27:32.400
Rob Ferguson: Yes, exactly.

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:34.140
Daniel Han: You can, like,
not even train some layers.

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:37.620
Like, you can just train
the last layer with rank 1.

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:38.070
Rob Ferguson: Right.

00:27:38.070 --> 00:27:40.830
Daniel Han: And it should
still work very well.

00:27:40.830 --> 00:27:43.710
Rob Ferguson: And so, for
the audience who is learning

00:27:43.710 --> 00:27:45.900
and thinking about post-training
for their first time,

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:48.870
I think that they're probably
realizing now that it used

00:27:48.870 --> 00:27:51.900
to be people would, anytime they
were talking about fine-tuning

00:27:51.900 --> 00:27:55.350
or anything at model weight
layer, it was sort of the thing

00:27:55.350 --> 00:28:00.120
for only the world's top ML
teams, incredibly expensive.

00:28:00.120 --> 00:28:03.330
What opportunities does this
create for people who are going

00:28:03.330 --> 00:28:05.910
into post-training
for the first time?

00:28:05.910 --> 00:28:07.680
Daniel Han: Fireworks
has a very good system

00:28:07.680 --> 00:28:08.670
to do training as well.

00:28:08.670 --> 00:28:10.350
Like, Unsloth also has
an open-source repo

00:28:10.350 --> 00:28:12.480
to do training --
Mark is from PyTorch!

00:28:12.480 --> 00:28:15.780
So I guess we all hope that
we try our best to make the,

00:28:15.780 --> 00:28:16.350
you know, experience

00:28:16.350 --> 00:28:18.570
for everyone much more
better and easier.

00:28:18.570 --> 00:28:19.710
And I feel like all the tools

00:28:19.710 --> 00:28:22.050
that we have now are
just very powerful now.

00:28:22.050 --> 00:28:22.260
Right?

00:28:22.260 --> 00:28:22.860
So like, for example,

00:28:22.860 --> 00:28:26.340
Unswap reduces memory usage
by, you know, 70% to 90%.

00:28:26.340 --> 00:28:27.600
We make it much faster.

00:28:27.600 --> 00:28:29.760
And our goal was to make
it on local computers.

00:28:29.760 --> 00:28:31.470
So like, you know, essentially,

00:28:31.470 --> 00:28:34.560
by combining all the
optimizations from every single,

00:28:34.560 --> 00:28:38.100
you know, industry, from every
single corner of, like, PyTorch,

00:28:38.100 --> 00:28:40.199
you know, wherever we can
find "enable-torch-compile".

00:28:40.233 --> 00:28:41.888
You know, like,
do lots of things,

00:28:41.921 --> 00:28:44.220
you can essentially
make training much easier.

00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:47.220
And now, once you make
the training much better,

00:28:47.220 --> 00:28:49.500
now it becomes, you know,
the practitioner's job.

00:28:49.500 --> 00:28:52.110
You know, what is the reward
function you want to design?

00:28:52.110 --> 00:28:54.150
You know, what is the
data set you want to use?

00:28:54.150 --> 00:28:56.400
Which model would
you want to utilize?

00:28:56.400 --> 00:28:57.540
How do we export it?

00:28:57.540 --> 00:28:59.760
You know, where do
we serve this model?

00:28:59.760 --> 00:29:02.310
And so, these extra
questions come.

00:29:02.310 --> 00:29:05.970
And so, like, I feel like,
you know, we try our best

00:29:05.970 --> 00:29:08.430
to make the infrastructure very
good and the software very good

00:29:08.464 --> 00:29:10.804
and easy and very
comfortable to use.

00:29:10.838 --> 00:29:12.030
But then, you know,

00:29:12.030 --> 00:29:14.220
the other questions
will also come.

00:29:14.220 --> 00:29:16.020
And so, we also have, for
example, have notebooks.

00:29:16.020 --> 00:29:18.090
We have a few
notebooks on our repo.

00:29:18.090 --> 00:29:19.890
How do we do a
reinforcement learning?

00:29:19.890 --> 00:29:23.400
And, yeah, there's a lot
of examples as well, yeah.

00:29:23.400 --> 00:29:24.720
Rob Ferguson: So I love that.

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:25.770
I'd love to talk
a little bit more

00:29:25.770 --> 00:29:27.660
about optimization of inference.

00:29:27.660 --> 00:29:30.630
And so, most teams
building on top

00:29:30.630 --> 00:29:32.520
of these frameworks
are absorbing gains

00:29:32.520 --> 00:29:34.908
from flash attention,
speculative coding,

00:29:34.942 --> 00:29:36.783
and kernel-level optimization

00:29:36.816 --> 00:29:37.874
without understanding

00:29:37.907 --> 00:29:40.280
the trade-offs
underneath them at all.

00:29:41.250 --> 00:29:43.920
That matters because the
hardware is changing fast.

00:29:43.920 --> 00:29:46.290
And the teams that understand
what is actually happening

00:29:46.290 --> 00:29:50.100
at the kernel layer will know
what holds and what does not

00:29:50.100 --> 00:29:52.050
as these changes land.

00:29:52.050 --> 00:29:55.740
Mark has worked at this layer
longer than almost anyone,

00:29:55.740 --> 00:29:58.410
including co-authoring
Kernel LLM

00:29:58.410 --> 00:30:02.160
to start automating the kernel
generation process itself.

00:30:02.160 --> 00:30:05.100
That creates a genuinely
interesting tension.

00:30:05.100 --> 00:30:07.860
Mark knows better than almost
anyone what hand-optimized

00:30:07.860 --> 00:30:10.350
kernels could do, and he
is also building tools

00:30:10.350 --> 00:30:12.660
to automate it away.

00:30:12.660 --> 00:30:15.600
Why don't you walk us through
what is actually happening

00:30:15.600 --> 00:30:18.450
when these optimizations run
and what teams are giving

00:30:18.450 --> 00:30:20.700
up when they abstract over them?

00:30:20.700 --> 00:30:21.840
Mark Saroufim: Oof, yeah.

00:30:21.840 --> 00:30:23.670
It's a big question.

00:30:23.670 --> 00:30:25.360
Where do I start?

00:30:26.670 --> 00:30:27.720
Okay, yeah.

00:30:27.720 --> 00:30:29.850
So I will first off, like,
start off by saying, like,

00:30:29.850 --> 00:30:32.790
open-source, like,
infrastructure is quite good.

00:30:32.790 --> 00:30:35.190
So like, basically, the way I
would think of this is, like,

00:30:35.190 --> 00:30:38.700
if you're just starting out, you
can just use the managed vendor.

00:30:38.700 --> 00:30:40.860
Like, that gives you, like,
either training or inference

00:30:40.860 --> 00:30:43.140
as a service, and they'll
charge you something.

00:30:43.140 --> 00:30:44.520
Right?

00:30:44.520 --> 00:30:47.130
And then, like, that time,

00:30:47.130 --> 00:30:49.170
you can normalize it
based on GPU costs.

00:30:49.170 --> 00:30:51.300
So if you look at the
average cost of a GPU,

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:53.610
it's going to be $2 to $8 or so.

00:30:53.610 --> 00:30:55.830
And so, now, like, that
gives you the baseline

00:30:55.830 --> 00:30:57.240
for what you're being charged.

00:30:57.240 --> 00:30:59.970
And then just use the
open-source thing and see, like,

00:30:59.970 --> 00:31:01.650
basically what the margins are.

00:31:01.650 --> 00:31:03.660
And you can use this as,
like, negotiation leverage

00:31:03.660 --> 00:31:06.150
versus any vendor that
you're using and just try

00:31:06.150 --> 00:31:08.760
to understand a bit better,
like, how much, like,

00:31:08.760 --> 00:31:10.890
would it have been possible
for you in a reasonable amount

00:31:10.890 --> 00:31:13.170
of time to get this perf game?

00:31:13.170 --> 00:31:14.610
So what are the
core tricks, right?

00:31:14.610 --> 00:31:16.770
Like, the core tricks,
like, for the most part,

00:31:16.770 --> 00:31:19.620
when you're working with GPUs
and PyTorch, for instance,

00:31:19.620 --> 00:31:21.660
PyTorch was very, very popular

00:31:21.660 --> 00:31:24.120
because PyTorch supports
print statements.

00:31:24.120 --> 00:31:26.940
So you can, like, run a piece of
code and then you can print it.

00:31:26.940 --> 00:31:29.460
This was a revolution because
most other previous ML

00:31:29.460 --> 00:31:31.830
frameworks did not support
this because they were, like,

00:31:31.830 --> 00:31:34.500
much more based off
of graph capture.

00:31:34.500 --> 00:31:37.530
But having, like, eager
execution has a huge tradeoff

00:31:37.530 --> 00:31:39.960
in that, like, you're basically,
let's say you're reading a value

00:31:39.960 --> 00:31:42.510
from memory, you're
doing some computation,

00:31:42.510 --> 00:31:44.610
then you write the
value back to memory,

00:31:44.610 --> 00:31:45.810
and then you read it again,

00:31:45.810 --> 00:31:47.910
and then you keep doing
this back and forth.

00:31:47.910 --> 00:31:51.210
And each round trip to global
memory on a GPU is going to be,

00:31:51.210 --> 00:31:53.040
like, 500 cycles or so.

00:31:53.040 --> 00:31:55.470
So this is why fusion
compilers, like Torch Compile,

00:31:55.470 --> 00:31:57.570
became very popular
because you can do this

00:31:57.570 --> 00:31:59.130
in one cycle instead.

00:31:59.130 --> 00:32:01.440
So if you just make sure
that your code is fused,

00:32:01.440 --> 00:32:03.240
that's what I would say
is the number one sort

00:32:03.240 --> 00:32:05.550
of most important
thing you should do.

00:32:05.550 --> 00:32:08.310
Number two is, like,
launch overhead.

00:32:08.310 --> 00:32:10.230
So if you look at,
like, things like MOEs,

00:32:10.230 --> 00:32:13.020
the shapes get quite small, and
so the bottom, like, becomes,

00:32:13.020 --> 00:32:16.065
like, just running, waiting
for a function to be queued

00:32:16.099 --> 00:32:17.782
to the GPU. And this is where

00:32:17.815 --> 00:32:20.070
techniques like CUDA
Graphs are quite popular.

00:32:20.070 --> 00:32:22.980
So fusions plus CUDA Graphs
is what I would argue, like,

00:32:22.980 --> 00:32:26.520
80% of the value that any
managed provider is giving you,

00:32:26.520 --> 00:32:28.980
and that last 20%
is "secret sauce".

00:32:28.980 --> 00:32:30.270
So what's the "secret sauce"?

00:32:30.270 --> 00:32:32.820
The secret sauce is
typically kernels.

00:32:32.820 --> 00:32:36.060
Kernels are functions
that run on a GPU.

00:32:36.060 --> 00:32:38.490
They're very, like,
time-consuming to write

00:32:38.490 --> 00:32:40.320
and hard to get right.

00:32:40.320 --> 00:32:41.580
The main benefit of them is

00:32:41.580 --> 00:32:44.580
that they will either
make code faster

00:32:44.580 --> 00:32:47.370
or they'll make code
take less memory.

00:32:47.370 --> 00:32:49.440
But if it takes less
memory, that's also great

00:32:49.440 --> 00:32:51.720
because you can increase
your batch size

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:53.040
and make code run faster.

00:32:53.040 --> 00:32:54.360
So like, memory and, like,

00:32:54.360 --> 00:32:56.310
speed are very much,
like, intertwined.

00:32:56.310 --> 00:32:58.560
So this is where I would say,
like, fairly time-consuming.

00:32:58.560 --> 00:33:00.780
And, you know, it used to
be when I worked at Meta,

00:33:00.780 --> 00:33:02.820
there was, like, sort of
a small class of people

00:33:02.820 --> 00:33:04.440
that would write
this kind of code.

00:33:04.440 --> 00:33:06.540
They were, like, kind of grumpy,
they sit in a corner.

00:33:06.540 --> 00:33:08.190
They don't really interact
with, like, people much.

00:33:08.190 --> 00:33:09.060
Rob Ferguson: They
work at Fireworks.

00:33:09.060 --> 00:33:10.590
Mark Saroufim: Yeah, so now
they work at Fireworks.

00:33:10.590 --> 00:33:12.660
I see the team out there.

00:33:12.660 --> 00:33:15.360
So like, so this is great.

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:18.570
But my observation basically
later was, like, okay, well,

00:33:18.570 --> 00:33:21.660
yes, it's hard, but people are
curious about these problems.

00:33:21.660 --> 00:33:23.940
And this, like, very much,
like, motivated the creation

00:33:23.940 --> 00:33:25.980
of communities like GPU
Mode where we're, like,

00:33:25.980 --> 00:33:27.630
look, we know this is hard.

00:33:27.630 --> 00:33:29.910
We know we won't get the same
purpose that experts are going

00:33:29.910 --> 00:33:31.200
to get, but we don't care.

00:33:31.200 --> 00:33:33.420
We're going to reinvent
the wheel anyways.

00:33:33.420 --> 00:33:35.880
And it turns out, like, it's
hard, but it's not, like,

00:33:35.880 --> 00:33:37.890
as hard as you might
think it is, right?

00:33:37.890 --> 00:33:41.640
So I think this created a
whole boom of projects, like,

00:33:41.640 --> 00:33:44.880
both at the sort of GPU-rich
scale and the GPU-poor scale

00:33:44.880 --> 00:33:47.190
where people are writing
faster and faster GPU code.

00:33:47.190 --> 00:33:50.520
And, basically, the lessons were
being shared by kernel libraries

00:33:50.520 --> 00:33:53.340
and tutorials,
kernel competitions.

00:33:53.340 --> 00:33:55.320
Where it got really interesting
is, like, basically,

00:33:55.320 --> 00:33:59.550
even in 2025, AI models really,
really sucked at, like,

00:33:59.550 --> 00:34:02.520
writing GPU code, even
though it's verifiable.

00:34:02.520 --> 00:34:03.660
But there were signs of life.

00:34:03.660 --> 00:34:05.850
Like, basically, there were
signs that, like, huh,

00:34:05.850 --> 00:34:07.590
like the models were
getting a bit better

00:34:07.590 --> 00:34:10.080
at writing Trident code
if you sampled a lot.

00:34:10.080 --> 00:34:11.220
They were getting a bit better

00:34:11.220 --> 00:34:13.020
if you showed them more
examples and context.

00:34:13.020 --> 00:34:15.390
It would get a bit
better if you fine-tuned

00:34:15.390 --> 00:34:17.550
or if you ran an RL
post-training run.

00:34:17.550 --> 00:34:19.950
But, like, once you did,
you're like, ah, like,

00:34:19.950 --> 00:34:22.530
I can look at problems that
would be too time-consuming

00:34:22.530 --> 00:34:25.230
to write a kernel for and
I can use an AI model.

00:34:25.230 --> 00:34:27.000
I think this has been
revolutionary for, like,

00:34:27.000 --> 00:34:29.490
basically educating
people and getting them

00:34:29.490 --> 00:34:31.250
to write, like, more kernels.

00:34:31.250 --> 00:34:33.710
And I think the frontier now,
for me at least, has become,

00:34:33.710 --> 00:34:36.620
like, okay, like, it's
fairly self-evident

00:34:36.620 --> 00:34:39.540
that we can write GPU kernels
for a fast, let's say,

00:34:39.540 --> 00:34:41.370
fused RMS norm kernel.

00:34:41.370 --> 00:34:42.990
It's not at all self-evident

00:34:42.990 --> 00:34:46.830
that we'll have an AI write a
faster matrix multiplication

00:34:46.830 --> 00:34:50.190
kernel because matrix
multiplication is the most

00:34:50.190 --> 00:34:52.530
optimized algorithm
in human history.

00:34:52.530 --> 00:34:53.550
We've been chugging along

00:34:53.550 --> 00:34:56.120
at this algorithm
for over 200 years.

00:34:56.120 --> 00:34:58.460
The hardware changes every,
like, 18 months or so,

00:34:58.460 --> 00:35:00.170
so we re-optimize it again.

00:35:00.170 --> 00:35:02.510
So this is what I think is,
like, a very important, like,

00:35:02.510 --> 00:35:05.270
frontier for AI model and one
that we're quite excited about,

00:35:05.270 --> 00:35:08.430
like, companies
such as Core Auto.

00:35:08.430 --> 00:35:12.230
Rob Ferguson: In fact, Daniel
hand-wrote Triton optimization.

00:35:12.230 --> 00:35:12.980
Daniel Han: Yes.

00:35:12.980 --> 00:35:15.620
Yes. That was back in the --

00:35:15.620 --> 00:35:16.310
Rob Ferguson: The Dark Ages.

00:35:16.310 --> 00:35:17.520
Daniel Han: Yes.

00:35:18.560 --> 00:35:22.050
I think as Mark said,
you can get 80%

00:35:22.050 --> 00:35:23.760
of the way there
with Torch Compile.

00:35:23.760 --> 00:35:24.120
Rob Ferguson: Yep.

00:35:24.120 --> 00:35:25.020
Daniel Han: I feel
like the problem

00:35:25.020 --> 00:35:28.020
with Torch Compile is it
actually has a lot of flags,

00:35:28.020 --> 00:35:33.090
and you can enable lots of
things that it's very hard.

00:35:33.090 --> 00:35:35.430
My view was, like, maybe they
should expose all the flags

00:35:35.430 --> 00:35:37.950
and tell everyone, okay, this
flag is here, this flag is --

00:35:37.950 --> 00:35:40.140
like, for example, there's
coordinate descent tuning.

00:35:40.140 --> 00:35:44.100
It can maybe increase
performance by, like, 1% to 5%.

00:35:44.100 --> 00:35:46.380
So like, how do we turn
that on, for example?

00:35:46.380 --> 00:35:47.520
There's another
thing which I really

00:35:47.520 --> 00:35:49.080
like called combo kernels,

00:35:49.080 --> 00:35:51.000
where you confuse the
kernels together.

00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:53.670
Like, there's, like, separate
kernels that don't look

00:35:53.670 --> 00:35:56.900
like they're the same thing,
but you can combine them

00:35:56.900 --> 00:35:58.790
and execute them all together.

00:35:58.790 --> 00:36:01.190
And I don't think that's
enabled by default.

00:36:01.190 --> 00:36:03.050
Mark Saroufim: No, the
unfortunate thing is, like,

00:36:03.050 --> 00:36:05.377
most of these flags
are in a file called:

00:36:05.410 --> 00:36:13.590
"torch-_inductor/_config.py"
They're all there.

00:36:13.590 --> 00:36:15.330
But it's just, like --

00:36:15.330 --> 00:36:16.800
it's a good file, though.

00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:17.280
It's a good file.

00:36:17.280 --> 00:36:17.880
Daniel Han: Yes, exactly.

00:36:17.880 --> 00:36:19.230
Just read that file.

00:36:19.230 --> 00:36:21.270
Read that file and, like,
just turn on all that,

00:36:21.270 --> 00:36:22.590
you know, try all of them.

00:36:22.590 --> 00:36:23.670
There's a lot of combinations.

00:36:23.670 --> 00:36:26.160
Maybe do, like, some binary
search or something.

00:36:26.160 --> 00:36:29.340
And like, my view is,
like, you can get a lot

00:36:29.340 --> 00:36:31.250
of performance by doing that.

00:36:31.250 --> 00:36:32.930
But I feel like, you
know, as Mark said,

00:36:32.930 --> 00:36:35.690
matrix multiplication is a
very complicated algorithm.

00:36:35.690 --> 00:36:38.300
You know, humans have been
doing this for, like, ages.

00:36:38.300 --> 00:36:40.890
I don't know what's the last
optimization, like, N --

00:36:40.890 --> 00:36:42.270
Mark Saroufim: Is
that the final one

00:36:42.270 --> 00:36:44.280
that we do before
computers figure it out?

00:36:44.280 --> 00:36:45.540
Daniel Han: Oh,
yeah, maybe, yeah.

00:36:45.540 --> 00:36:47.010
Like, it's very,
very complicated.

00:36:47.010 --> 00:36:49.890
Like, even, you know,
Strassen and, you know --

00:36:49.890 --> 00:36:51.540
even if you invent
a good algorithm,

00:36:51.540 --> 00:36:54.420
it's also good to run as well.

00:36:54.420 --> 00:36:55.560
Like, Strassen, for example.

00:36:55.560 --> 00:36:57.120
It maybe could be useful,

00:36:57.120 --> 00:36:58.500
but maybe it's not
that useful, right?

00:36:58.500 --> 00:37:00.810
Because, like, GPUs
have caching, you know,

00:37:00.810 --> 00:37:02.640
different layers of
memory hierarchy,

00:37:02.640 --> 00:37:04.590
and it's not that effective.

00:37:04.590 --> 00:37:07.740
But maybe, maybe reinforcement
learning might find something

00:37:07.740 --> 00:37:10.200
interesting, like, you know,
exploiting the cache hierarchy

00:37:10.200 --> 00:37:11.760
or something like that.

00:37:11.760 --> 00:37:12.690
But I feel like maybe --

00:37:12.690 --> 00:37:14.460
do you think Flash
Attention could be written

00:37:14.460 --> 00:37:16.140
by -- I feel like that's.

00:37:16.140 --> 00:37:16.380
Mark Saroufim: Yeah.

00:37:16.380 --> 00:37:18.060
Daniel Han: And it didn't
see Flash Attention.

00:37:18.060 --> 00:37:20.010
Do you think it could,
like, invent that?

00:37:20.010 --> 00:37:23.520
Mark Saroufim: So okay, well,
like, I might eat my words here,

00:37:23.520 --> 00:37:26.850
but, like, I do suspect Flash
Attention 5 won't be written

00:37:26.850 --> 00:37:27.540
by a human.

00:37:27.540 --> 00:37:27.810
Daniel Han: Oh.

00:37:27.810 --> 00:37:28.350
Mark Saroufim: So this is, like,

00:37:28.350 --> 00:37:30.570
my bold prediction,
but we'll see.

00:37:30.570 --> 00:37:33.120
I mean, you can remind me
if I'm wrong in 18 months.

00:37:33.120 --> 00:37:34.590
Rob Ferguson: Okay, next Build.

00:37:34.590 --> 00:37:34.980
Mark Saroufim: Yes.

00:37:34.980 --> 00:37:37.350
Rob Ferguson: We'll look
at Flash Attention 5.

00:37:37.350 --> 00:37:39.360
Daniel Han: But I
feel like algorithms,

00:37:39.360 --> 00:37:40.500
like gradient checkpointing.

00:37:40.500 --> 00:37:40.770
Rob Ferguson: Yeah.

00:37:40.770 --> 00:37:42.540
Daniel Han: If the model
never knew what gradient

00:37:42.540 --> 00:37:45.780
checkpointing was, it probably
could figure out, okay,

00:37:45.780 --> 00:37:47.070
let's do gradient checkpointing.

00:37:47.070 --> 00:37:51.360
But it's such a
fantastic algorithm.

00:37:51.360 --> 00:37:52.254
Like, I wouldn't even call --

00:37:52.287 --> 00:37:55.284
Is it a technique or algorithm?

00:37:55.317 --> 00:37:57.750
Like, it's just very hard
to invent these things.

00:37:57.750 --> 00:37:59.384
And I feel like
humans have this, like,

00:37:59.417 --> 00:38:01.230
you know, I wouldn't
say creativity,

00:38:01.230 --> 00:38:04.710
more like they think
outside the box much more.

00:38:04.710 --> 00:38:06.168
And I feel like -- so
AI models definitely

00:38:06.201 --> 00:38:07.260
can think outside the box.

00:38:07.260 --> 00:38:08.280
They can still do that, right?

00:38:08.280 --> 00:38:10.439
Because they prod
every single, you know --

00:38:10.472 --> 00:38:12.230
if you give it enough time,
it will probably figure it out.

00:38:12.230 --> 00:38:13.040
Rob Ferguson: Yeah,
if you're getting

00:38:13.040 --> 00:38:14.360
into simulations of things.

00:38:14.360 --> 00:38:14.990
Daniel Han: Yes, exactly.

00:38:14.990 --> 00:38:17.600
Rob Ferguson: But the
transformer model, generally,

00:38:17.600 --> 00:38:18.590
it's trying to figure out how

00:38:18.590 --> 00:38:21.570
to transpose something it's seen
somewhere or someplace else.

00:38:21.570 --> 00:38:24.150
And so, if it has a
memory of this thing,

00:38:24.150 --> 00:38:25.980
then it can try to transpose it.

00:38:25.980 --> 00:38:32.310
But we still find that often
we need to open up other --

00:38:32.310 --> 00:38:33.780
Daniel Han: Yeah,
handcraft stuff, yeah.

00:38:33.780 --> 00:38:36.450
Like, flash attention,
gradient checkpointing.

00:38:36.450 --> 00:38:38.177
These are, like, so powerful.

00:38:38.599 --> 00:38:40.495
You know, it
revolutionized the industry,

00:38:40.529 --> 00:38:42.960
just these two, for example.

00:38:42.960 --> 00:38:45.840
Yeah. I guess, yeah,
Flash Attention 5

00:38:45.840 --> 00:38:47.550
and 6 and 7 or whatever.

00:38:47.550 --> 00:38:48.330
Yeah, okay.

00:38:48.330 --> 00:38:50.430
Mark Saroufim: Yeah, I mean,
I certainly agree with you.

00:38:50.430 --> 00:38:52.680
Like, I think from a
capability perspective, like,

00:38:52.680 --> 00:38:54.480
where I've seen people
get the most sort of bang

00:38:54.480 --> 00:38:58.470
for their buck is, like, let's
say, getting coverage of kernels

00:38:58.470 --> 00:38:59.880
on alternative hardware.

00:38:59.880 --> 00:39:00.030
Daniel Han: Oh.

00:39:00.030 --> 00:39:02.190
Mark Saroufim: So like, let's
say you're using a welcome chip

00:39:02.190 --> 00:39:04.410
where there isn't, like, a
big community around it.

00:39:04.410 --> 00:39:06.810
Then you can use an AI and it
can, like, one-shot a kernel.

00:39:06.810 --> 00:39:09.030
But it can really one-shot it
because there's just, like,

00:39:09.030 --> 00:39:11.480
not enough humans on the
planet that can do it.

00:39:11.480 --> 00:39:13.760
And the sort of, like, the
entry level to get, like,

00:39:13.760 --> 00:39:15.230
something decent isn't that high

00:39:15.230 --> 00:39:18.590
because the human attention
isn't all that high either.

00:39:18.590 --> 00:39:19.820
But, like, once you
get to sort of, like,

00:39:19.820 --> 00:39:22.310
problems where it's been
very competitive for humans

00:39:22.310 --> 00:39:25.170
and you look at, like, let's
say, AI-generated kernels,

00:39:25.170 --> 00:39:26.760
today they are still slop.

00:39:26.760 --> 00:39:28.500
Like, they will be very long.

00:39:28.500 --> 00:39:30.090
They won't be very clear.

00:39:30.090 --> 00:39:31.860
They'll have a lot of fallbacks.

00:39:31.860 --> 00:39:34.500
They'll fall back to
PyTorch a lot and give up.

00:39:34.500 --> 00:39:37.140
If you push it even harder,
it'll, like, reward hack.

00:39:37.140 --> 00:39:41.340
So like, we're not there yet, I
think, for the hardest problems.

00:39:41.340 --> 00:39:43.500
But if your problem looks
like I have this, like,

00:39:43.500 --> 00:39:47.220
variant of an RMS norm that
kind of looks like an RMS norm

00:39:47.220 --> 00:39:49.530
but isn't quite one, and
I can look at, like,

00:39:49.530 --> 00:39:53.010
Daniel's RMS Trident norm kernel
and change it, yes, like,

00:39:53.010 --> 00:39:56.310
AI will 100% like one-shot
like these things today.

00:39:56.310 --> 00:39:58.370
Rob Ferguson: Okay.
Before we get into rotations

00:39:58.403 --> 00:39:59.970
and other kernel optimizations,

00:39:59.970 --> 00:40:01.620
I think we'll up-level
a little bit.

00:40:01.620 --> 00:40:06.480
So speculative decoding assumes
a stable output distribution.

00:40:06.480 --> 00:40:09.570
As workloads shift towards
long-running agentic tasks

00:40:09.570 --> 00:40:11.310
with variable output lengths,

00:40:11.310 --> 00:40:13.920
where does that assumption
start to break?

00:40:13.920 --> 00:40:15.810
Mark Saroufim: I don't know.

00:40:15.810 --> 00:40:19.140
I haven't run spec-decoding
much, like, in prod, but, like,

00:40:19.140 --> 00:40:22.597
the way I would figure it's sort
of, like, the same, as in, like,

00:40:23.250 --> 00:40:24.300
you're not going to
be making, like,

00:40:24.300 --> 00:40:26.130
very long-running,
like, predictions.

00:40:26.130 --> 00:40:29.700
Like, those will probably
be quite brittle.

00:40:29.700 --> 00:40:31.105
But if, like, let's
say you, like,

00:40:31.950 --> 00:40:33.570
assume this is an
interactive workload

00:40:33.570 --> 00:40:37.610
and you're basically reconsuming
all the past generated tokens,

00:40:37.610 --> 00:40:39.380
I think speculative dec
should probably continue

00:40:39.380 --> 00:40:41.390
to survive for a long
time as a technique.

00:40:41.390 --> 00:40:44.210
I think what makes speculative
decoding really powerful,

00:40:44.210 --> 00:40:45.990
I think, as a
technique, is that, like,

00:40:45.990 --> 00:40:48.270
it basically takes workloads

00:40:48.270 --> 00:40:51.090
that have the performance
characteristic of decode

00:40:51.090 --> 00:40:53.910
and turn it into the performance
characteristic of pre-fill,

00:40:53.910 --> 00:40:56.610
which is, like, always going
to be something GPUs want.

00:40:56.610 --> 00:41:00.030
And so, as a result, while,
like, it might not be as useful

00:41:00.030 --> 00:41:03.000
or a bit more useful or a bit
less useful, I just, like,

00:41:03.000 --> 00:41:05.700
am highly suspicious that the
technique would ever die.

00:41:05.700 --> 00:41:06.930
Like, I think in
some sense, like,

00:41:06.930 --> 00:41:10.560
that plus disaggregated
serving, like,

00:41:10.560 --> 00:41:13.410
techniques that really help,
like, bump up the, like,

00:41:13.410 --> 00:41:15.780
NVIDIA stock, like, I
think they're very key

00:41:15.780 --> 00:41:18.300
to the chip being
irrelevant in the future.

00:41:18.300 --> 00:41:20.850
Daniel Han: If the acceptance
rate, like, drops, like,

00:41:20.850 --> 00:41:23.040
if your distribution
changes too much,

00:41:23.040 --> 00:41:25.110
then the acceptance rates
will drop sometimes.

00:41:25.110 --> 00:41:25.980
And so maybe you might have

00:41:25.980 --> 00:41:28.290
to recalibrate your
speculative decoding head

00:41:28.290 --> 00:41:29.010
and stuff like that.

00:41:29.010 --> 00:41:30.930
So I guess you will have
to, like, re-fine-tune

00:41:30.930 --> 00:41:32.790
on the new distribution.

00:41:32.790 --> 00:41:34.620
But yes, over time,
you can, like,

00:41:34.620 --> 00:41:37.230
the acceptance rate will drop
as reinforcement learning,

00:41:37.230 --> 00:41:39.060
okay -- I'm just guessing.

00:41:39.060 --> 00:41:41.460
But generally, the
acceptance rate will drop.

00:41:41.460 --> 00:41:44.370
And then you can, like, you
know, fine-tune it back up.

00:41:44.370 --> 00:41:47.070
Yeah, it's just, like,
more efficiency, I guess.

00:41:47.070 --> 00:41:47.790
Yeah.

00:41:47.790 --> 00:41:49.020
Rob Ferguson: Absolutely.

00:41:49.020 --> 00:41:52.560
I just think that, you
know, we, I think just

00:41:52.560 --> 00:41:54.510
in the last two years,
we've seen a big change.

00:41:54.510 --> 00:41:56.670
You know, we had what
I would call "talkers";

00:41:56.670 --> 00:42:00.530
people basically would do
one command back and forth.

00:42:00.530 --> 00:42:02.450
And then all of a sudden, you
get into the agentic world

00:42:02.450 --> 00:42:06.530
where it's 40 to 100 for every
single task that's there.

00:42:06.530 --> 00:42:08.120
And it's very, very
hard for people

00:42:08.120 --> 00:42:09.860
to understand what drops off

00:42:09.860 --> 00:42:13.340
when you send something
100 times away as, like,

00:42:13.340 --> 00:42:16.040
where are the trade-offs
that are starting to form?

00:42:16.040 --> 00:42:17.171
And at what layer

00:42:17.204 --> 00:42:19.710
could those trade-offs
actually start to form in?

00:42:19.710 --> 00:42:23.760
And so I suspect that any
optimization that's going

00:42:23.760 --> 00:42:26.970
to maintain coherence or be able

00:42:26.970 --> 00:42:31.260
to have longer lengths matters
more and more as we get

00:42:31.260 --> 00:42:33.090
into agentic workloads
on the other side.

00:42:33.090 --> 00:42:34.770
Daniel Han: Yeah, in, like,
long context, like, you know,

00:42:34.770 --> 00:42:36.900
models are getting better
and better at long context.

00:42:36.900 --> 00:42:38.460
But that is also a problem.

00:42:38.460 --> 00:42:41.900
Like, if your, you know, run
is very, very, very long

00:42:41.900 --> 00:42:43.550
and your Claude
Code or, you know,

00:42:43.550 --> 00:42:47.510
whatever codex run is very long,
it will get dumber and dumber

00:42:47.510 --> 00:42:49.490
and dumber and dumber over time
as you have to do compaction

00:42:49.490 --> 00:42:50.600
or somewhere like that.

00:42:50.600 --> 00:42:52.190
And then how do
we do compaction?

00:42:52.190 --> 00:42:56.580
You know, like, which details
do we compact or remove?

00:42:56.580 --> 00:42:59.940
It is a very complicated
engineering problem now.

00:42:59.940 --> 00:43:04.920
I'm sure everyone's trying
to figure out this as well.

00:43:04.920 --> 00:43:06.690
Rob Ferguson: Do you feel it's
also, like, a math problem?

00:43:06.690 --> 00:43:09.780
Just like, if you're
soft maxing over, like,

00:43:09.814 --> 00:43:12.703
a very large denominator, like,
all your values will be,

00:43:12.763 --> 00:43:15.420
like, close to zero anyways,
and it's just noise.

00:43:15.420 --> 00:43:17.100
Do you feel like
there's any ways out?

00:43:17.100 --> 00:43:19.050
Like, any hot takes on soft max?

00:43:19.050 --> 00:43:19.800
Daniel Han: I don't know.

00:43:19.800 --> 00:43:21.600
Somehow remove it and
do something else,

00:43:21.600 --> 00:43:22.680
like linear or something.

00:43:22.680 --> 00:43:23.580
Rob Ferguson: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:43:23.580 --> 00:43:24.840
Daniel Han: Like, exponential.

00:43:24.840 --> 00:43:26.580
Well, I guess soft
max is, I don't know,

00:43:26.580 --> 00:43:28.110
exponentially weighted somehow.

00:43:28.110 --> 00:43:28.500
I don't know.

00:43:28.500 --> 00:43:31.020
Just some dynamic
weighting system.

00:43:31.020 --> 00:43:32.400
Sounds more complicated, though.

00:43:32.400 --> 00:43:32.640
Rob Ferguson: Yeah.

00:43:32.640 --> 00:43:34.230
Daniel Han: And then the
loss function has to change.

00:43:34.230 --> 00:43:34.860
Rob Ferguson: Yes.

00:43:34.860 --> 00:43:37.550
Daniel Han: And then that's
even more problematic.

00:43:37.550 --> 00:43:38.870
How do we do that?

00:43:38.870 --> 00:43:40.010
Rob Ferguson: Yeah, there's
a lot of open questions,

00:43:40.010 --> 00:43:41.510
I feel, for a very long time.

00:43:41.510 --> 00:43:42.170
Daniel Han: Delete the soft max,

00:43:42.170 --> 00:43:44.540
do linear, and
see what happens.

00:43:44.540 --> 00:43:45.920
But then I don't know
how to do the prediction

00:43:45.920 --> 00:43:46.730
of the next token, though.

00:43:46.730 --> 00:43:47.450
What do we do about that?

00:43:47.450 --> 00:43:50.510
Okay, so it's a bit
more complicated.

00:43:50.510 --> 00:43:52.020
Okay, fine.

00:43:52.020 --> 00:43:53.310
Rob Ferguson: Okay, so
there's much more I'd love

00:43:53.310 --> 00:43:55.050
to talk about, but we're
going to be coming

00:43:55.050 --> 00:43:56.250
up on time pretty soon.

00:43:56.250 --> 00:43:59.640
So I think I'll up-level my
last question for both of you.

00:43:59.640 --> 00:44:01.800
So you know, we've talked
to both a little bit

00:44:01.800 --> 00:44:04.140
about training and system-side.

00:44:04.140 --> 00:44:05.970
Both of you have described
layers of the stack

00:44:05.970 --> 00:44:08.370
that were built for a
world that is changing.

00:44:08.370 --> 00:44:10.410
So this is my question to close.

00:44:10.410 --> 00:44:12.300
Given everything that
we've discussed,

00:44:12.300 --> 00:44:15.120
where is the highest-leverage
place for a builder in this room

00:44:15.120 --> 00:44:16.890
to put their energy right now?

00:44:16.890 --> 00:44:19.650
And what are they going
to waste their time on?

00:44:19.650 --> 00:44:21.840
Mark Saroufim: I have a
hot take on this question.

00:44:21.840 --> 00:44:24.240
Which is, like, I think, like,

00:44:24.240 --> 00:44:27.660
agents exacerbate, like, any
sort of Amdahl's law effects.

00:44:27.660 --> 00:44:30.450
As in, like, let's say you
have an agent running an

00:44:30.450 --> 00:44:31.530
inference server,

00:44:31.530 --> 00:44:33.330
it's not going to get
bottle-necked by the inference,

00:44:33.330 --> 00:44:34.830
it's going to get bottle-necked
by the time it takes

00:44:34.830 --> 00:44:37.560
to start the server, which
is, like, bottlenecked

00:44:37.560 --> 00:44:40.050
by the time it takes to
warm up CUDA graphs,

00:44:40.050 --> 00:44:42.060
by the time it takes to
JIT compile kernels,

00:44:42.060 --> 00:44:45.060
by the time it takes to
read things from a disk,

00:44:45.060 --> 00:44:47.400
by the time it takes to do,
like, multi-threaded, like,

00:44:47.400 --> 00:44:50.190
reads back to your checkpoints.

00:44:50.190 --> 00:44:53.040
So like, I think what I found
very interesting about, like,

00:44:53.040 --> 00:44:55.560
agents is that, like,
the bottlenecks might be

00:44:55.560 --> 00:44:56.880
in sort of strange places.

00:44:56.880 --> 00:44:59.670
So if you're, like, generally
passionate about systems,

00:44:59.670 --> 00:45:01.260
I think you'll find something

00:45:01.260 --> 00:45:02.850
that agents will find
very, very useful.

00:45:04.011 --> 00:45:07.560
Daniel Han: My take is,
like, similar, but, like,

00:45:07.560 --> 00:45:11.190
do less, do the maths first.

00:45:11.190 --> 00:45:14.280
Like, you know, how did gradient
checkpointing come along?

00:45:14.280 --> 00:45:16.500
You know, why was flash
attention invented?

00:45:16.500 --> 00:45:19.200
You know, what is fused
cross-entropy loss

00:45:19.200 --> 00:45:20.130
or something like that?

00:45:20.130 --> 00:45:21.390
You know, what is standby mode?

00:45:21.390 --> 00:45:22.620
You know, something like that.

00:45:22.620 --> 00:45:24.390
And then work on kernels.

00:45:24.390 --> 00:45:26.310
So like, most people in
my view, like, you know,

00:45:26.310 --> 00:45:28.290
you will start
working on kernels,

00:45:28.290 --> 00:45:30.000
I would say take an
opposite approach.

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:33.210
You know, do the maths,
do the algorithms first,

00:45:33.210 --> 00:45:35.850
and then think, you know,
how to do the kernels.

00:45:35.850 --> 00:45:39.930
And then definitely look at
the TorchCompile config file,

00:45:39.930 --> 00:45:41.790
you know, read all of it.

00:45:41.790 --> 00:45:44.070
You know, ask Claude
or whatever codex.

00:45:44.070 --> 00:45:45.510
Read it through all.

00:45:45.510 --> 00:45:47.670
You know, see which
flags are useful.

00:45:47.670 --> 00:45:50.790
Do binary search and whatever.

00:45:50.790 --> 00:45:52.290
Not binary search.

00:45:52.290 --> 00:45:54.960
Like, binary grouping, like,
you know, enable 50% of it

00:45:54.960 --> 00:45:57.390
and then see, okay, the other
50%, which one's faster.

00:45:57.390 --> 00:45:58.290
And, you know, so on.

00:45:58.290 --> 00:46:00.240
Oh, wait, that is binary search.

00:46:00.240 --> 00:46:01.140
Bisectional or whatever.

00:46:01.140 --> 00:46:02.940
I think it's called
bisectional or something.

00:46:02.940 --> 00:46:04.740
But, yes, do that, and
it will be very useful.

00:46:04.740 --> 00:46:06.120
Yeah.

00:46:06.120 --> 00:46:06.510
Rob Ferguson: All right.

00:46:06.510 --> 00:46:09.300
Well, I really want to
thank everybody for coming

00:46:09.300 --> 00:46:12.030
and hearing us learn out
about kernels today.

00:46:12.030 --> 00:46:17.760
And definitely check out both
Daniel at Unsloth and Mark

00:46:17.760 --> 00:46:19.950
at Core Automate
now that it's public.

00:46:19.950 --> 00:46:21.300
Mark Saroufim: Oh, yeah.

00:46:21.300 --> 00:46:23.730
Rob Ferguson: And as well as
learn more about Fireworks.

00:46:23.730 --> 00:46:25.320
Here's information
about our session.

00:46:25.320 --> 00:46:27.780
And we'll try to hang out to
answer any questions you have.

00:46:27.780 --> 00:46:28.530
Thanks so much.

00:46:28.530 --> 00:46:28.920
Daniel Han: Thank you.

00:46:28.920 --> 00:46:29.490
Mark Saroufim: Thank you, folks.

00:46:29.490 --> 00:46:30.020
Thank you.

00:46:30.020 --> 00:46:31.824
